      "writtenOn": "2022-06-13T22:46:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "With `conn.set_signal_match_mode(true)`, we should actually always return `true` and never returns `false`, because each signal handler always gets a chance to process a signal that it registers. Since we use it this way I think that the value of using the Enum is not apparent anymore? If this is to make the code more readable, should we instead add a comment somewhere to always return true?\n\nIf you still think it\u0027s more readable to use an enum, `true` should be `Stop` and `false` should be called `Continue` (but again `Continue` does not really make sense here because the signal is not shared among multiple handlers, so there is nothing to \"continue\").",
      "parentUuid": "d5352c10_cf56ac39",
      "revId": "bf42d014ef623edb109806c01dbcd3bb92bb514d",
